<section class="p-hero"><div class="p-hero-img"><img src="/assets/img/photos/wildfire-ruins.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/wildfire-ruins-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/wildfire-ruins.webp 1023w" sizes="100vw" width="1023" height="683" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap p-hero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/disputed-claim-services/">Services</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Wildfire Total Loss Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Wildfire Total Loss Appraisal for California Homeowners</h1><p class="lede">When a wildfire takes the whole house, the dispute is usually about what it costs to rebuild today, under today’s codes, in a market where every builder is booked. We price that rebuild.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-copper" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/get-in-touch/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Recent fires</dt><dd>Camp, Tubbs, Palisades, Eaton</dd></div><div><dt>Key limits</dt><dd>Dwelling, ERC, code, debris</dd></div><div><dt>Rebuild window</dt><dd>Often 36 months+</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>California’s wildfire losses</h2>
<p>The 2017 Tubbs Fire destroyed thousands of homes in Santa Rosa. The 2018 Camp Fire leveled most of Paradise in Butte County, and the Woolsey Fire burned through Malibu and parts of Ventura County the same week. In January 2025, the Palisades and Eaton fires destroyed whole neighborhoods in Pacific Palisades and Altadena. After each, the same question came up for thousands of families: is the policy enough to rebuild?</p>
<h2>What a wildfire total loss appraisal covers</h2>
<p>When the home is destroyed, the amount of loss is the cost to rebuild a comparable home, subject to the policy limits. Common areas of disagreement include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cost per square foot:</strong> generic rebuild figures often miss local labor rates, hillside foundations, custom finishes and post-disaster demand surges.</li>
<li><strong>Extended replacement cost:</strong> many California policies add 20% to 50% or more above the dwelling limit if the rebuild costs more. Whether you can reach it depends on the estimate.</li>
<li><strong>Building code upgrades:</strong> fire-resistant construction, fire sprinklers in some jurisdictions, energy code and accessibility requirements.</li>
<li><strong>Debris removal:</strong> some owners opt into government debris programs, others hire private contractors. The cost and the coverage interact.</li>
<li><strong>Other structures and landscaping:</strong> separate limits, often under-valued.</li>
</ul>
<h2>California protections worth knowing</h2>
<p>California law gives wildfire victims added protections after a declared state of emergency. Among others, Insurance Code §2051.5 generally allows at least 36 months to collect full replacement cost in those cases, with extensions for good cause, and lets owners rebuild or buy elsewhere without the carrier deducting the value of the land. Rules on contents advances and additional living expenses have also been expanded and continue to change. These are legal questions, so confirm the current rule with a California attorney or the Department of Insurance.</p>
<h2>Contents</h2>
<p>Building a contents inventory from memory after a total loss is exhausting. Many carriers now accept inventories with less detail in declared disasters, and some advance a portion of contents limits. We can help organize an inventory and value it, and our <a href="/claim-check-tools/rebuild-cost-underinsurance-check/">rebuild cost check</a> can show whether your dwelling limit is likely to be enough.</p>
<h2>FAIR Plan homes</h2>
<p>Many hillside and rural homes are insured through the California FAIR Plan for fire, often with a separate difference-in-conditions policy for other perils. FAIR Plan policies are typically written on an actual cash value basis unless replacement cost was purchased, which changes the math considerably.</p>

<h2>Questions about wildfire total loss appraisal</h2><div class="qa"><details><summary>Can I use appraisal on a total loss?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>Yes, if the carrier accepts the loss but disputes the rebuild cost or other amounts. It cannot raise your policy limits, but it can determine whether the loss reaches them.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do I have to rebuild on the same lot?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>California law generally allows rebuilding or buying elsewhere after a declared-disaster total loss, but how that affects the payment depends on the policy. Ask before you decide.</p></div></details><details><summary>How long do I have to rebuild?<svg class="ic chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="qa-a"><p>In declared states of emergency, California generally allows at least 36 months to collect replacement cost, with possible extensions. Confirm the current rule for your loss.</p></div></details></div>
